{"module":"policing","voice":"male","duration":225.0,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Policing and the 911 call","start":0.0,"end":2.74,"words":[[0.05,0.58],[0.58,0.8],[0.8,0.95],[0.95,2.38],[2.38,2.6]]},{"el":"intro","text":"The CAREN Act is about one specific harm: calling the police on someone because of their race.","start":3.54,"end":9.1,"words":[[3.59,3.73],[3.73,4.09],[4.09,4.3],[4.3,4.45],[4.45,4.81],[4.81,5.02],[5.02,5.59],[5.59,5.88],[6.13,6.49],[6.49,6.63],[6.63,6.99],[6.99,7.13],[7.13,7.56],[7.56,7.92],[7.92,8.06],[8.06,8.27],[8.27,8.63]]},{"el":"intro","text":"This module shows where that harm comes from and why a phone call can be dangerous.","start":9.45,"end":15.01,"words":[[9.5,9.74],[9.74,10.23],[10.23,10.55],[10.55,10.79],[10.79,11.03],[11.03,11.28],[11.28,11.6],[11.6,11.92],[11.92,12.16],[12.16,12.41],[12.41,12.57],[12.57,12.89],[12.89,13.13],[13.13,13.38],[13.38,13.54],[13.54,14.43]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Slave patrols","start":15.36,"end":17.3,"words":[[15.41,16.06],[16.06,17.1]]},{"el":"s0","text":"From South Carolina's 1704 law onward, Southern colonies and states organized slave patrols: white men who rode at night, stopped Black people, demanded passes, searched slave quarters and whipped people found without permission.","start":18.0,"end":34.58,"words":[[18.05,18.38],[18.38,18.71],[18.71,19.52],[19.52,20.51],[20.51,20.67],[20.67,21.16],[21.45,21.86],[21.86,22.43],[22.43,22.68],[22.68,23.17],[23.17,23.82],[23.82,24.23],[24.23,24.97],[25.26,25.58],[25.58,25.83],[25.83,25.99],[25.99,26.32],[26.32,26.48],[26.48,26.89],[27.18,27.59],[27.59,27.92],[27.92,28.41],[28.69,29.35],[29.35,29.84],[30.13,30.54],[30.54,30.95],[30.95,31.44],[31.44,31.68],[31.68,32.01],[32.01,32.42],[32.42,32.83],[32.83,33.32],[33.32,33.97]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Every white person was expected to help police enslaved people.","start":34.93,"end":38.92,"words":[[34.98,35.25],[35.25,35.52],[35.52,35.86],[35.86,36.07],[36.07,36.68],[36.68,36.82],[36.82,37.09],[37.09,37.43],[37.43,37.97],[37.97,38.38]]},{"el":"h1","text":"From patrols to police","start":39.82,"end":41.61,"words":[[39.86,40.19],[40.19,40.83],[40.83,41.0],[41.0,41.4]]},{"el":"s1","text":"After emancipation, many Southern patrol duties passed to new city police and sheriffs.","start":42.31,"end":48.45,"words":[[42.36,42.67],[42.67,43.69],[43.97,44.28],[44.28,44.67],[44.67,45.22],[45.22,45.69],[45.69,46.0],[46.0,46.16],[46.16,46.39],[46.39,46.7],[46.7,47.09],[47.09,47.33],[47.33,47.88]]},{"el":"s1","text":"How direct the line is from patrols to modern policing is debated: Northern departments (Boston 1838, New York 1845) grew mostly from London's model and from controlling immigrants and labor.","start":48.8,"end":61.75,"words":[[48.84,49.05],[49.05,49.53],[49.53,49.67],[49.67,49.94],[49.94,50.08],[50.08,50.35],[50.35,50.9],[50.9,51.04],[51.04,51.38],[51.38,51.86],[51.86,52.0],[52.0,52.62],[52.86,53.2],[53.2,53.89],[53.89,54.3],[54.3,55.26],[55.5,55.71],[55.71,55.91],[55.91,56.8],[57.04,57.25],[57.25,57.73],[57.73,58.01],[58.01,58.49],[58.49,58.83],[58.83,59.04],[59.04,59.31],[59.31,60.0],[60.0,60.61],[60.61,60.82],[60.82,61.23]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Historians agree that in the South, the job of controlling Black movement carried over.","start":62.1,"end":67.92,"words":[[62.14,62.89],[62.89,63.2],[63.2,63.42],[63.42,63.58],[63.58,63.73],[63.73,64.1],[64.37,64.52],[64.52,64.82],[64.82,64.97],[64.97,65.73],[65.73,66.03],[66.03,66.56],[66.56,66.94],[66.94,67.32]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Black Codes, vagrancy laws and convict leasing","start":68.82,"end":71.86,"words":[[68.86,69.13],[69.13,69.55],[69.79,70.42],[70.42,70.63],[70.63,70.84],[70.84,71.32],[71.32,71.67]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The 13th Amendment (1865) abolished slavery “except as a punishment for crime.”","start":72.56,"end":78.65,"words":[[72.61,72.75],[72.75,73.18],[73.18,73.83],[73.83,74.91],[75.16,75.66],[75.66,76.23],[76.23,76.67],[76.67,76.81],[76.81,76.95],[76.95,77.6],[77.6,77.74],[77.74,78.17]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Southern states immediately passed Black Codes making it a crime for Black people to be unemployed, to walk at night, or to “loiter.”","start":79.0,"end":88.02,"words":[[79.03,79.44],[79.44,79.94],[79.94,80.76],[80.76,81.09],[81.09,81.41],[81.41,81.83],[81.83,82.32],[82.32,82.48],[82.48,82.65],[82.65,83.06],[83.06,83.22],[83.22,83.55],[83.55,83.96],[83.96,84.13],[84.13,84.29],[84.29,85.11],[85.4,85.56],[85.56,85.81],[85.81,85.97],[85.97,86.38],[86.67,86.75],[86.75,86.92],[86.92,87.41]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Those arrested were leased to plantations, mines and railroads, often dying at higher rates than under slavery.","start":88.37,"end":96.38,"words":[[88.41,88.74],[88.74,89.3],[89.3,89.46],[89.46,89.78],[89.78,89.94],[89.94,90.91],[91.19,91.59],[91.59,91.83],[91.83,92.64],[92.92,93.24],[93.24,93.64],[93.64,93.8],[93.8,94.12],[94.12,94.53],[94.53,94.77],[94.77,95.09],[95.09,95.81]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Convict leasing lasted in Alabama until 1928.","start":96.73,"end":100.96,"words":[[96.78,97.29],[97.29,97.65],[97.65,98.09],[98.09,98.24],[98.24,98.74],[98.74,99.11],[99.11,100.41]]},{"el":"h3","text":"An accusation could be a death sentence","start":101.86,"end":104.64,"words":[[101.91,102.08],[102.08,102.96],[102.96,103.22],[103.22,103.4],[103.4,103.57],[103.57,103.83],[103.83,104.44]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Many lynchings began with an accusation from a white person.","start":105.34,"end":109.34,"words":[[105.38,105.66],[105.66,106.23],[106.23,106.58],[106.58,106.8],[106.8,106.94],[106.94,107.65],[107.65,107.94],[107.94,108.08],[108.08,108.36],[108.36,108.79]]},{"el":"s3","text":"In 1955 Carolyn Bryant's account of an encounter in a Mississippi store led to the murder of 14-year-old Emmett Till.","start":109.69,"end":118.87,"words":[[109.74,109.91],[109.91,111.29],[111.29,111.9],[111.9,112.58],[112.58,113.1],[113.1,113.27],[113.27,113.45],[113.45,114.14],[114.14,114.31],[114.31,114.48],[114.48,115.17],[115.17,115.43],[115.43,115.69],[115.69,115.86],[115.86,116.03],[116.03,116.38],[116.38,116.55],[116.55,117.67],[117.67,118.01],[118.01,118.36]]},{"el":"s3","text":"(In a 2017 book, historian Timothy Tyson reported that she later admitted parts of her account were untrue; her family disputes this.","start":119.22,"end":129.13,"words":[[119.27,119.43],[119.43,119.59],[119.59,120.71],[120.71,121.03],[121.31,122.02],[122.02,122.5],[122.5,122.98],[122.98,123.54],[123.54,123.78],[123.78,123.94],[123.94,124.34],[124.34,124.9],[124.9,125.21],[125.21,125.37],[125.37,125.53],[125.53,126.01],[126.01,126.17],[126.17,126.65],[126.93,127.09],[127.09,127.57],[127.57,128.2],[128.2,128.52]]},{"el":"s3","text":"That detail is debated.)","start":129.48,"end":131.18,"words":[[129.53,129.71],[129.71,130.09],[130.09,130.21],[130.21,130.77]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The lesson the CAREN Act builds on is older than any single case: when the person called about is Black, the police response can be deadly.","start":131.53,"end":140.2,"words":[[131.58,131.74],[131.74,132.11],[132.11,132.27],[132.27,132.64],[132.64,132.87],[132.87,133.25],[133.25,133.4],[133.4,133.55],[133.55,133.93],[133.93,134.16],[134.16,134.38],[134.38,134.84],[134.84,135.22],[135.48,135.71],[135.71,135.86],[135.86,136.24],[136.24,136.54],[136.54,136.92],[136.92,137.07],[137.07,137.45],[137.71,137.87],[137.87,138.24],[138.24,138.77],[138.77,139.0],[139.0,139.15],[139.15,139.61]]},{"el":"h4","text":"The weaponized 911 call today","start":141.1,"end":143.8,"words":[[141.14,141.27],[141.27,141.86],[141.86,143.08],[143.08,143.28],[143.28,143.6]]},{"el":"s4","text":"In 2018 and 2020, videos showed white callers phoning police about Black people barbecuing (Oakland), waiting in a Starbucks (Philadelphia, where two men were arrested), selling water (San Francisco) and birdwatching (Central Park).","start":144.5,"end":161.14,"words":[[144.54,144.7],[144.7,145.65],[145.65,145.89],[145.89,146.92],[147.2,147.83],[147.83,148.15],[148.15,148.46],[148.46,148.86],[148.86,149.34],[149.34,149.73],[149.73,150.13],[150.13,150.45],[150.45,150.84],[150.84,151.56],[151.56,152.19],[152.47,152.94],[152.94,153.1],[153.1,153.26],[153.26,153.82],[153.82,154.69],[154.97,155.2],[155.2,155.36],[155.36,155.6],[155.6,155.76],[155.76,156.39],[156.67,157.07],[157.07,157.39],[157.39,157.62],[157.62,158.42],[158.69,158.93],[158.93,159.72],[159.72,160.28],[160.28,160.6]]},{"el":"s4","text":"San Francisco passed the CAREN Act in 2020, following Oregon's 2019 law and California's AB 1775.","start":161.49,"end":170.35,"words":[[161.52,161.74],[161.74,162.5],[162.5,162.8],[162.8,162.95],[162.95,163.33],[163.33,163.55],[163.55,163.7],[163.7,164.68],[164.95,165.47],[165.47,166.0],[166.0,166.98],[166.98,167.13],[167.13,167.36],[167.36,168.11],[168.11,168.26],[168.26,169.77]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The data backs up the videos.","start":170.7,"end":172.91,"words":[[170.74,170.87],[170.87,171.21],[171.21,171.48],[171.48,171.62],[171.62,171.76],[171.76,172.37]]},{"el":"s4","text":"In San Francisco, only about 1% of “suspicious person” calls in the past year led to an arrest, citation or report.","start":173.26,"end":184.1,"words":[[173.31,173.51],[173.51,173.81],[173.81,174.91],[175.26,175.76],[175.76,176.26],[176.26,177.15],[177.15,177.35],[177.35,178.15],[178.15,178.65],[178.65,179.05],[179.05,179.25],[179.25,179.45],[179.45,179.85],[179.85,180.15],[180.15,180.45],[180.45,180.65],[180.65,180.85],[180.85,181.44],[181.79,182.69],[182.69,182.79],[182.79,183.39]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The federal Police-Public Contact Survey (2020) found that Black people who had contact with police were nearly three times as likely as white people to experience threats or use of force.","start":184.45,"end":195.72,"words":[[184.5,184.64],[184.64,185.15],[185.15,185.95],[185.95,186.45],[186.45,186.82],[186.82,187.69],[187.94,188.3],[188.3,188.52],[188.52,188.81],[188.81,189.18],[189.18,189.32],[189.32,189.54],[189.54,190.05],[190.05,190.26],[190.26,190.63],[190.63,190.77],[190.77,191.13],[191.13,191.35],[191.35,191.71],[191.71,191.86],[191.86,192.3],[192.3,192.44],[192.44,192.73],[192.73,193.09],[193.09,193.24],[193.24,194.11],[194.11,194.47],[194.47,194.54],[194.54,194.76],[194.76,194.91],[194.91,195.2]]},{"el":"h5","text":"What works","start":196.62,"end":197.72,"words":[[196.67,197.04],[197.04,197.55]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Cities are sending trained responders instead of armed police to many calls.","start":198.42,"end":203.45,"words":[[198.47,198.88],[198.88,198.96],[198.96,199.45],[199.45,199.93],[199.93,200.66],[200.66,201.15],[201.15,201.31],[201.31,201.56],[201.56,201.96],[201.96,202.12],[202.12,202.45],[202.45,202.85]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Eugene, Oregon's CAHOOTS program has done so since 1989.","start":203.8,"end":209.03,"words":[[203.84,204.37],[204.64,205.17],[205.17,205.63],[205.63,206.24],[206.24,206.47],[206.47,206.69],[206.69,206.92],[206.92,207.23],[207.23,208.45]]},{"el":"s5","text":"A 2022 study in Science Advances found that Denver's STAR program reduced reports of the low-level crimes it targeted.","start":209.38,"end":217.84,"words":[[209.43,209.59],[209.59,210.65],[210.65,211.03],[211.03,211.18],[211.18,211.63],[211.63,212.24],[212.24,212.62],[212.62,212.84],[212.84,213.3],[213.3,213.53],[213.53,214.13],[214.13,214.66],[214.66,215.12],[215.12,215.27],[215.27,215.42],[215.42,216.03],[216.03,216.48],[216.48,216.63],[216.63,217.24]]},{"el":"s5","text":"A federal CAREN Act adds the other half: a consequence for using the police as a weapon.","start":218.19,"end":224.12,"words":[[218.24,218.39],[218.39,218.94],[218.94,219.33],[219.33,219.57],[219.57,219.8],[219.8,219.96],[219.96,220.19],[220.19,220.5],[220.78,220.93],[220.93,221.72],[221.72,221.87],[221.87,222.26],[222.26,222.42],[222.42,222.81],[222.81,222.97],[222.97,223.12],[223.12,223.59]]}]}